render/pixman: NV12 DMA-BUF textures

The pixman renderer has no DRM FD and no DMA-BUF texture formats, so a
compositor using it offers no linux-dmabuf, and no client buffer can be
scanned out directly: the DRM backend scans out DMA-BUFs only. On a
display controller whose plane takes YUV but which has no GPU, a video
client's NV12 frames could go straight to the plane, but have to be
converted and composited on the CPU instead.

Give the pixman renderer a duplicate of the backend's DRM FD, taken in
wlr_renderer_autocreate(), and DMA-BUF texture formats of NV12 with the
linear and implicit modifiers. A texture imported from an NV12 DMA-BUF
is converted to XRGB8888 (BT.601, limited range) on the CPU the first
time it is drawn or read, so a buffer that is scanned out is never even
mapped. DMA-BUF mappings are often write-combined, so the conversion
copies whole rows out with wide loads before converting them, with NEON
on aarch64 (bit-exact with the C code). A buffer that cannot be
converted is drawn black. RGB formats are not offered: they would only
move clients off wl_shm onto the same CPU copy.

With an FD, the renderer would also make DMA-BUF screen capture
reachable, which it cannot fill. ext-image-copy-capture and
wlr-screencopy now offer DMA-BUFs only if the renderer can render into
them.

Without a backend DRM FD (the headless backend, or the multi-GPU blit
renderer) nothing changes.

Signed-off-by: badgeos
